{"availability":"available","full":"amiothenes/auto-language-learning","name":"auto-language-learning","description":"A Next.js language learning application for tracking vocabulary growth at the lemma (root word) level.","category":"Education","language":"TypeScript","stars":1,"forks":0,"created":"2025-12-24T20:13:53Z","updated":"2026-10-08T22:56:30Z","branch":"master","homepage":"https://verbista.vercel.app","topics":[],"demo":"https://verbista.vercel.app/","color":"#193546","ink":"#a6d6e8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-08T23:43:00.159Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-08T23:43:00.159Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-08T23:43:00.159Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-08T23:43:00.159Z","stars":1,"forks":0}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["A language learning reader that tracks vocabulary at the lemma level, so your known-word count reflects what you actually know rather than how many surface forms you've encountered.","I was learning Russian and couldn't find a reader that tracked vocabulary at the lemma level. Existing tools counted word forms separately, which inflated known-word counts: \"идти,\" \"иду,\" and \"шёл\" would register as three distinct words instead of one. So I built a reader that treats them all as the same lemma and tracks progress accordingly."],"features":["Lemma-first vocabulary tracking: inflected forms resolve to their root, so \"шёл,\" \"иду,\" and \"идти\" all count toward the same word","Five-tier status system (Newly Seen, Familiar, Known, Well-Known, Ignored) with inline color-coded highlighting that does not interrupt reading flow","spaCy NLP pipeline handles tokenization, POS tagging, and lemmatization — results stored at import time for instant reader loads","Series support: group related texts and track cumulative vocabulary progress across a collection","Known-word percentage displayed per text and per series, updated as you interact with words"],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/amiothenes/auto-language-learning/b